{"name":"Rubidium","hex":"#c5b9b4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rubidium","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The chemical element (symbol Rb) with an atomic number of 37. It is a soft, highly reactive alkali metal.","A single atom of this element."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rubidium"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rubidium","blocks":[{"text":"Rubidium is a chemical element; it has symbol Rb and atomic number 37. It is a very soft, whitish-grey solid in the alkali metal group, similar to potassium and caesium. Rubidium is the first alkali metal in the group to have a density higher than water. On Earth, natural rubidium comprises two isotopes: 72% is a stable isotope, 85Rb, and 28% is the slightly radioactive 87Rb, with a half-life of 48.8 billion years – more than three times as long as the estimated age of the universe."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Rb5.JPG/330px-Rb5.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubidium"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/RbH.JPG/500px-RbH.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/RbH.JPG/1280px-RbH.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/RbH.JPG/500px-RbH.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Partially molten rubidium metal in an ampoule","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARbH.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Rb%26Cs_crystals.jpg/500px-Rb%26Cs_crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Rb%26Cs_crystals.jpg/1280px-Rb%26Cs_crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.